  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual overview of how genomic tools can support ecological restoration, shown by comparing a healthy reference ecosystem, a degraded one, and a partially restored system. On the left, the reference landscape has diverse trees, birds, and a rich soil community represented by many differently colored organisms and microbes. Degradation, driven by disturbance such as excavation or clearing, simplifies the habitat and visibly reduces aboveground life and belowground biodiversity. Restoration actions like planting and seeding then rebuild vegetation and begin to recover the broader biological community, though the restored system still looks intermediate between degraded and reference states.\n\nThe right side summarizes three genomic approaches that can guide this process. Population genomics for seed sourcing is presented as helping choose appropriate provenance, capture adaptive variation, and measure genetic diversity in restoration material. Meta-omics is shown as a way to assess recovery by tracking ecosystem components and ecological interaction networks, including plants, animals, and microbes. Genome editing is depicted as a more interventionist option, generating novel genotypes or gene drives to promote desired native traits or suppress invasive species. Overall, the figure emphasizes restoration as both rebuilding ecosystems and managing genetic and community diversity.",
